text:
Aisha: Customer reported duplicate billing when their client retried after a 429. They say they used the Node SDK defaults. Any immediate checks?

Tom: Do we have the request_id(s) from the customer or the account id? Need to see whether two successful completions were recorded server-side.

Claire: Billing shows two charge events at 14:03:12 and 14:03:18 UTC for acct_9xZ. Looks like two separate successful writes.

Ravi: Quick context on SDK defaults — by default our Node SDK retries on network errors, 5xx, and 429 with exponential backoff + full jitter. Default max_attempts is 4. Important: retries alone do not make a non-idempotent operation safe; customers need to send an Idempotency-Key header (or use the SDK idempotency option) for POST-style endpoints.

Tom: Server behavior note: for the async /jobs endpoints we return 202 + job id. If client retries without an idempotency key we treat it as a new job. For synchronous endpoints a duplicate successful result could also be billed twice if the retry hit after the first completion.

Aisha: Customer sent a repro snippet (they're using fetch + a retry loop). They didn't set any idempotency header.

```
// simplified repro from customer
async function send(prompt) {
  for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    try {
      const res = await fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/responses', {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: { 'Authorization': 'Bearer sk-XXX', 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
        body: JSON.stringify({ model: 'redwood-13b', input: prompt })
      });
      const j = await res.json();
      console.log('status', res.status, j);
      return j;
    } catch (err) {
      // naive immediate retry
      console.log('retry', i, err.message);
    }
  }
}
```

Ravi: That code illustrates the issue. Suggestions: 1) Add an Idempotency-Key header (UUID) per logical operation. 2) Let the SDK handle retries (it will attach idempotency if you use the SDK helper) OR set max_attempts=1 for non-idempotent calls. 3) For customers using manual retry loops, implement exponential backoff + jitter and send Idempotency-Key.

Example curl with idempotency header:
```
curl -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/responses \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-XXX" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -H "Idempotency-Key: 2f1d4b72-9c4a-4e1b-8b3d-123456789abc" \
  -d '{"model":"redwood-13b","input":"hi"}'
```

Claire: If server logs confirm two completions for different request_ids and no idempotency key, we should offer a refund for the duplicate charge. Please attach the two request ids and timestamps.

Tom: From infra: grep the access logs for acct_9xZ around 14:03 and include correlation ids. Example command: `grep acct_9xZ /var/log/redwood/access.log | sed -n '1,200p'`. Also check `response_status` and `processing_time_ms`.

quota-bot: Account acct_9xZ current quota: daily_tokens=1,000,000; burst_window=10,000/min; quota_reset=00:00 UTC. No quota exhaustion events recorded at 14:03.

Aisha: Customer also showed their client received a 429 then retried immediately and later got a 200. They didn't see a 202 flow. They want guidance on SDK config and whether defaults are safe.

Ravi: Actionable guidance to give customer: 1) Set an idempotency key for each logical request. 2) If you must roll-your-own retry loop, use exponential backoff + full jitter and limit attempts (e.g., max 3). 3) Prefer letting our SDK handle retries; if using SDK, set `sdkConfig.maxAttempts` and enable `sdkConfig.attachIdempotency: true` if available. 4) For payment/billing sensitive flows, avoid automatic retries entirely and implement client-side dedupe with idempotency.

Tom: Internally — let's add a note in the SDK README and the quickstart about idempotency for POST endpoints and an example with curl + Node.

Claire: I'll hold on refund until we get the two request ids. If infra confirms two accepted completions and no idempotency header, we proceed with refund.

Aisha: I'll ask the customer for the two request ids and share logs when received.

Ravi: I'll open a small docs PR with examples and a short SDK flag to explicitly disable retries for non-idempotent endpoints. ETA: tomorrow EOD.

Tom: Thanks — pls keep this thread updated. :thumbsup:
